WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.037 --> 00:00:10.560
- So I'm gonna be talking
this morning about

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.040
an introduction to Autism.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.290
This is only an introduction,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.020
we're gonna hit it very lightly.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.320
But I am delighted to be here to talk

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.060
and we'll have a chance, as I mentioned,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.470
for this group to ask questions later.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.240
I should just mention my
conflicts of interest,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.960
which are a grant of which Kasia is

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.200
the principle investigator
long with a number of books

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.636
and I also edit the Journal of Autism.

00:00:29.636 --> 00:00:31.820
This book in particular I would mention,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.080
whoops, and I lost that book

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.400
'cause I did something to the page.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.400
Oh my gosh, oh, please don't start on me.

00:00:40.013 --> 00:00:42.703
Slideshow, back to this current slide.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.900
That's what I get for
messing with the thing

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.210
I'm not gonna touch anymore.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.430
The book at the top corner of the right

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.330
is a third edition of
a book that originally

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:54.738
got started for my undergraduate class

00:00:54.738 --> 00:00:57.580
from Cambridge University
Press and it's just come out

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.303
in the third edition, and
it's relatively inexpensive.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.692
So I wanna talk a little bit about autism

00:01:03.692 --> 00:01:06.210
in terms of as a diagnostic concept,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.060
a little bit of an overview as a field,

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.640
and something about
diagnosis, and then moving on

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.280
to key findings from research,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.800
and then talk about
outcomes and treatment,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.576
and then finally, our current needs

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.505
and some social policy issues,
which is a fascinating area.

00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:20.911
So development of the field.

00:01:20.911 --> 00:01:24.320
The origins of this really antedate

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.150
both Kanner and Asperger.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.920
If you look starting with
the the work of Itard

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.610
In France at the end of the 17th century,

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.747
early 18th century, there's
reports of Victor the Wild Boy,

00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:37.550
interest in so-called feral children,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.452
children who presumably were
being reared by animals,

00:01:39.452 --> 00:01:43.460
most of those children
probably had autism.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.820
If you read the reports of Victor,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.620
he had no language, had a
lot of unusual behaviors,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.410
he probably either eloped from his parents

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.370
or was abandoned and
then showed up at Itard,

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.560
that people assume this romantic notion

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.293
of animals taking care of children

00:01:56.293 --> 00:01:57.720
if they were found in the wild.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.540
Which I can tell you did not happen,

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.135
they would be food.

00:02:01.135 --> 00:02:04.583
And point of fact, with the
possible exception of dogs,

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.010
animals are not gonna treat
children like animals.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.170
But it's interesting
that we probably can see

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.730
the first reports of children
with autism at that point.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:17.220
Then if in the 1800s, and the
book on the top corner here,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.270
In a Different Key, is a
wonderful history of autism.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:22.644
These people tracked down
Kanner's first patient,

00:02:22.644 --> 00:02:25.097
Donald, who was still alive at that point

00:02:25.097 --> 00:02:27.561
and may still be, he's in his 80s if so.

00:02:27.561 --> 00:02:29.560
And they tracked him
down and talked to him,

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.040
but they also pointed out that the 1800s,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.050
some of the institutions in Massachusetts

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.760
that took children with
what we now would think of

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.468
as intellectual disability,
mental retardation,

00:02:37.468 --> 00:02:40.199
many of the cases describe
the cases where it sounds

00:02:40.199 --> 00:02:42.210
like the child had autism.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.670
From the point of who,
however. were actually

00:02:45.605 --> 00:02:46.610
defining autism, first describing it,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.140
the credit goes to Leo Kanner,

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.890
the First American Child Psychiatrist,

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.157
a refugee from Germany.

00:02:52.157 --> 00:02:55.030
He was at Hopkins and
he talked about 11 cases

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.759
of children who had two things,

00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:59.500
one was autism, living in their own world,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.357
not being socially connected.

00:03:01.357 --> 00:03:04.090
And the other thing was what
he called resistance to change,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.550
and we'll talk more about that.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.550
Resistance to change actually
meant several things.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.670
It meant literal trouble with change.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.420
Having trouble, having to
take the same route everyday

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.160
to go to the big Y or stop and shop,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.781
and deviation the kid fell apart.

00:03:15.781 --> 00:03:19.225
Same thing with changing around
the furniture in the room.

00:03:19.225 --> 00:03:21.040
Kanner mentioned some of his patients,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.790
as they would come back
would be upset with him

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.088
because he had changed how
his office was arranged.

00:03:25.088 --> 00:03:28.550
But this insistence on sameness also meant

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.468
a tendency for the child to use routines,

00:03:32.468 --> 00:03:35.479
and Kanner actually as part
of that same package included

00:03:35.479 --> 00:03:38.120
some of the unusual
behaviors and mannerisms,

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.500
the stereotype body
movements, the hand flapping.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.061
He saw that as an attempt
on the child's part

00:03:43.061 --> 00:03:46.060
to maintain sameness in the environment.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.530
So the interesting thing here is

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.238
he's talking about this
interesting paradox.

00:03:49.238 --> 00:03:51.090
On the one hand these are children who

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.120
are not socially clued in,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.550
but they're hyper clued in to
the non-social environment.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.150
And we're gonna try to
understand more about that

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.850
as we talk today.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.034
A year later, Han Asperger
who is a medical student

00:04:02.034 --> 00:04:05.343
in Vienna and during the
war he was in Austria,

00:04:05.343 --> 00:04:07.811
in Nazi-occupied Austria,

00:04:07.811 --> 00:04:10.010
and he talked about a group
of boys who had something

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.571
he called autistic personality disorder.

00:04:12.571 --> 00:04:16.140
Again, it's been translated
various ways from German,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.067
that's probably the best
way to translate it.

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:21.158
But these were boys who had
self-centered qualities,

00:04:21.158 --> 00:04:24.710
but they didn't show up until
the kids entered preschool

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.400
when they would hug
inappropriately other kids

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.760
who didn't know who they were.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.517
These were boys who had unusual interests,

00:04:29.517 --> 00:04:32.561
they often pursued their
interest with great intensity

00:04:32.561 --> 00:04:34.759
to the point where it interfered

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:36.400
with their learning of other things,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.320
which is a key point, 'cause
it's that interference

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.597
with learning that moves
it to being a disorder.

00:04:40.597 --> 00:04:42.847
And he also pointed out that the families

00:04:42.847 --> 00:04:45.854
had revolved, their family life revolved

00:04:45.854 --> 00:04:47.360
around the child's special interests.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.300
If the kid was interested in trains,

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.750
they have to go to the train station

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:54.670
to see the 6:15 from
Lexington get in on time.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.570
And so he talked about these kids,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.950
again, as having autistic
personality problems.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.206
He did not see this as a
developmental disorder.

00:05:01.206 --> 00:05:03.870
Effectively, these two
descriptions set the stage

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.036
for what's a continuing debate,

00:05:06.036 --> 00:05:08.556
the tension between the broad
view and the narrow view.

00:05:08.556 --> 00:05:11.130
The broad view as we'll talk about

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.936
is supported somewhat by the genetics,

00:05:12.936 --> 00:05:14.950
which has identified many genes,

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.380
which can mean many gene variance,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.030
which means there's probably a lot of

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.010
other combination of genes out there

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.700
where people are maybe a little strange

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.370
or a little hyperfocused,
but they don't have autism.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.020
On the contrast, the narrow
view, the Kanner view

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.489
is a classic childhood autism,

00:05:29.489 --> 00:05:32.000
and this is the tension
that continues today.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.879
We'll talk a little bit about it with DSM.

00:05:33.879 --> 00:05:38.573
So the big accomplishments,
1950s and '60s,

00:05:38.573 --> 00:05:40.680
quite frankly, not much happened.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.430
Things started to happen in the '70s

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:44.880
as children with autism were followed.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.650
In the '50s and '60s, as we'll talk about

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.090
with the DSM, these were
children who were regarded

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:50.680
as having childhood schizophrenia.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.100
And that was because that word autism,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.956
people are, ah, autism, we know that word.

00:05:54.956 --> 00:05:56.305
It's one of the four A's that Bleuler used

00:05:56.305 --> 00:05:57.640
to describe schizophrenia.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.470
He talked about self-centered thinking.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:00.790
That's not what was
being talked about here.

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.780
These were children
who came into the world

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.480
without a predisposition
to connect with people.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.090
But, that was a term that was used.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.440
It was very much impossible
to sort all this out

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.430
because it of children with
childhood schizophrenia,

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.620
which turns out to be extremely rare.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.650
But people really had no good ideas

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.780
when the sad thing is, unfortunately,

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:20.480
people in the '50s and
'60s, as we'll talk about,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.817
started blaming the parents
for the child's problem.

00:06:22.817 --> 00:06:24.970
And we'll talk about the history of this,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.120
which is very interesting.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.017
In the 1970s, however,
several things happened.

00:06:29.017 --> 00:06:32.056
One was an awareness as children
were followed over time,

00:06:32.056 --> 00:06:34.367
they had a very high risk for seizures.

00:06:34.367 --> 00:06:37.400
About 20% of children with classic autism

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.281
will develop seizures of various kinds,

00:06:39.281 --> 00:06:42.037
usually major motor grand mal seizures,

00:06:42.037 --> 00:06:43.740
at various point in development,

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.730
early childhood and also in adolescence.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.918
Otherwise, this is an extremely rare time

00:06:47.918 --> 00:06:50.311
for people to first develop seizures.

00:06:50.311 --> 00:06:52.600
The other thing was in the late '70s,

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:53.981
the first twin study came out.

00:06:53.981 --> 00:06:56.222
There had been a paper a few months before

00:06:56.222 --> 00:06:58.056
by two prominent geneticists that said

00:06:58.056 --> 00:06:59.595
there's no genetics to autism.

00:06:59.595 --> 00:07:01.820
And then the first twin study came out

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.920
which said there's tremendous genetics.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.900
The first twin study was
done in the United Kingdom,

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.920
Susan Folstein and Michael Rutter,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:10.810
they looked at same sex
fraternal twin pairs.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.140
And the reason they looked at same sex

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.180
is there's a gender
issue of bias in autism,

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.030
and then identical twin pairs,

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:18.820
and they discovered
among the identical twins

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:21.045
was an amazing highly concordant.

00:07:21.045 --> 00:07:23.027
So if you had an identical
twin with autism,

00:07:23.027 --> 00:07:25.103
you were very, very likely to have autism.

00:07:25.103 --> 00:07:26.903
Not so among fraternal twins

00:07:26.903 --> 00:07:29.098
and in their first study,
which was pretty small,

00:07:29.098 --> 00:07:31.383
the risk was about the
same as in the population.

00:07:31.383 --> 00:07:33.564
We actually know that it's
actually somewhat higher.

00:07:33.564 --> 00:07:37.040
There is increased risk
for fraternal twins,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.030
but not nearly as much as identical twins.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:42.610
And finally, in the 1970s,
the first studies came out

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.182
that showed structured
education behavioral approaches

00:07:44.182 --> 00:07:48.680
were more effective than
unstructured psychotherapy,

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.230
which until that time, had
been the only treatment.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.460
Let me say a word or two about DSM.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.293
DSM I and II are largely
of historical interest.

00:07:58.293 --> 00:07:59.980
They are very theoretically based,

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:01.480
they have their own biases.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.080
Part of the problem with
having a theoretically based

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.477
diagnostic system is, if you
don't buy into the theory,

00:08:06.477 --> 00:08:09.600
you're kind of screwed,
and that's a problem.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.010
And so the big change in DSM III,

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.273
which I give Bob Spitzer, whose
picture is here on the side,

00:08:15.273 --> 00:08:16.106
great credit for.

00:08:16.106 --> 00:08:18.580
Bob Spitzer was part of the
Washington University School

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.500
in St. Louis who moved to Columbia,

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.530
and they were really very
focused on developing

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.341
what they called research
diagnostic criteria.

00:08:24.341 --> 00:08:27.280
This was a real change in psychiatry.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.410
When DSM III came out,

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.427
it really revolutionized the field.

00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:33.828
In DSM III, autism was
included for the first time.

00:08:33.828 --> 00:08:36.020
It was called infantile autism,

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.010
it was grouped under a class of disorders

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.210
called Pervasive Developmental Disorders.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:41.990
They came up with this term out of nowhere

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:43.740
'cause it had no history.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:47.160
And in retrospect, they could
have used a better term,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.188
autism-related conditions,
but they didn't.

00:08:49.188 --> 00:08:51.506
They used this funny term,
Pervasive Developmental Disorder,

00:08:51.506 --> 00:08:54.370
but autism was included.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.330
The trouble with the first definition was

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:59.078
it very much was a definition
of Infantile Autism.

00:08:59.078 --> 00:09:02.209
It was monothetic, which
means you had to meet

00:09:02.209 --> 00:09:03.993
all the criteria, pervasive
lack of responsiveness.

00:09:03.993 --> 00:09:06.410
We know as children get older with autism,

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.790
they do respond, they do
develop some social skills,

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.490
especially if they have an intervention.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:11.920
Now often those are odd, blah blah blah,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.660
but it's not pervasive.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:15.680
So there was a funny
category in DSM III called

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.934
Residual Infantile Autism,
which was very problematic

00:09:17.934 --> 00:09:20.342
because the residual
problems weren't so residual

00:09:20.342 --> 00:09:23.122
when you met these kids
in adolescence and adults.

00:09:23.122 --> 00:09:26.306
So many major changes were
made in the next iteration,

00:09:26.306 --> 00:09:28.844
DSM-III-R, which came out fairly quickly.

00:09:28.844 --> 00:09:32.650
We moved from Infantile
Autism to Autistic Disorder,

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.330
and now all of a sudden we
have the polythetic description

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.410
of the diagnostic criteria.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.770
Some from A, some from B, some from C.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.710
Social, communication,
and resistance to change.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.020
And this was again a big change

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.967
'cause all of a sudden you can
have different combinations

00:09:47.967 --> 00:09:51.910
that gets you a diagnosis
of autistic disorder.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:55.193
It was much better in terms of
approaching the whole range.

00:09:55.193 --> 00:09:58.150
The syndrome probably overdiagnosed

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.068
among the more intellectually disabled.

00:10:00.068 --> 00:10:03.020
Then we had DSM-IV, this was done

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.140
in conjunction with ICD-10.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:07.266
ICD-10 is the world system of diagnosis,

00:10:07.266 --> 00:10:12.266
technically speaking, DSM is
regarded as a regional variant,

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:17.170
I believe, and of course
DSM has captured the field

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.530
and did back in 1980, so around the world

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.680
that's what people used, but ICD,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.880
the International
Classification of Diseases,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:24.950
which is based in Geneva as part

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:26.740
of the World Health Organization,

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.160
by treaty we're obligated

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.370
to have a connection with the ICD.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:31.810
And that means those funny codes

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:33.670
when you go to the hospital
and you have pneumonia,

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:35.688
there's a code, that's an ICD code.

00:10:35.688 --> 00:10:38.379
And so DSM is related to
ICD around these codes,

00:10:38.379 --> 00:10:41.492
but if we're doing the DSM and the ICD,

00:10:41.492 --> 00:10:44.617
at the time we were involved in doing it

00:10:44.617 --> 00:10:46.540
and I was in charge of the DSM part of it

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:49.717
and Mike Rutter was the ICD-10 part of it.

00:10:49.717 --> 00:10:51.670
And we agreed, this is crazy.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:53.600
We don't need to have
two diagnostic systems,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.460
one for America, one for
the rest of the world,

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.646
we need one system if
we can possibly do it.

00:10:57.646 --> 00:11:00.160
This had all kinds of complications

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.740
and other issues which I won't get into,

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.420
but anyway in the end, we did
a lot of bells and whistles

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.380
and we did a big field trial.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.700
Over 100 rated, nearly 1,000 cases,

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:11.973
20 some sites around the world over year,

00:11:11.973 --> 00:11:13.880
and we came up with a new definition

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:17.290
that was essentially the
same for DSM-IV and ICD-10.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.611
This was around for almost 20 years,

00:11:19.611 --> 00:11:22.550
and it worked extremely well,
as we'll see when we have,

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:25.772
I'll show you some research
data in terms of research papers

00:11:25.772 --> 00:11:29.223
that was just an explosion of research.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.580
Now let me say a word about DSM-V.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:36.933
And you can sort of get my
take on it up at the top.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.059
Unfortunately, I'm sorry to
say, DSM-V is well motivated.

00:11:40.059 --> 00:11:43.069
Early on I was part of the process

00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:44.692
and I, along with some
other people, resigned

00:11:44.692 --> 00:11:47.180
because we didn't like
the way it was going.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:50.250
They included a new category,
Autism Spectrum Disorder,

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.950
great in terms of the name change.

00:11:53.121 --> 00:11:55.040
Another new disorder, called
Social Communication Disorder,

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.410
which has basically no or
very little rational basis,

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.573
but it was sort of thrown in there I think

00:12:00.573 --> 00:12:02.210
because they were rightly worried about

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:03.610
not including some children.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.500
They were no longer a
subthreshold category.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:08.590
It's what we called in the past, PPDNOS,

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:11.247
or atypical autism, that's all gone.

00:12:11.247 --> 00:12:15.400
So the criteria, we had two
rather than three categories,

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:18.774
social communication and
Resistance to change.

00:12:18.774 --> 00:12:21.821
This was done on the basis of
looking at a big body of data

00:12:21.821 --> 00:12:24.980
from the ADOS, the ADI
doing factor analysis

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.290
and other kinds of things.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.568
The trouble with factor analysis,

00:12:28.568 --> 00:12:30.380
I can tell you this from
looking at the DSM-IV data,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:33.400
you can come up, I can tell
you with the DSM-IV data,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.700
you can have a two-factor
solution, a three-factor solution,

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.473
or a five-factor solution, take your pick

00:12:38.473 --> 00:12:40.900
depending on which you thought works best.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.800
Again, I don't wanna over simplify it,

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.977
but it is kind of you take your pick.

00:12:44.977 --> 00:12:48.177
And we went with the
three-factor in DSM-IV

00:12:48.177 --> 00:12:50.860
because we thought it
gave us more flexibility.

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:54.310
And they went with two
factors basically in DSM-V,

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:56.580
social communication and
resistance to change.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:59.753
We reduced our number of criteria,

00:12:59.753 --> 00:13:03.660
we had a number of criteria in DSM-IV

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.620
that I think over 2,000
combinations and permutations

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:10.596
could get you a diagnosis of
autistic disorder in DSM-IV.

00:13:10.596 --> 00:13:13.080
So whatever else was true,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:14.810
there were a lot of ways to get in.

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.795
All of a sudden we went from 2,000 to 12.

00:13:17.795 --> 00:13:19.570
And in some funny way,
that's all you need to know

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.063
in terms of what the
issues are with DSM-V.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:25.940
So there's some good news.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:28.314
The good news is the recognition
of disorder, that's great.

00:13:28.314 --> 00:13:32.030
The bad and unexpected news, perhaps,

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:34.186
is that despite the name change
to Autism Spectrum Disorder,

00:13:34.186 --> 00:13:37.370
the concept is more narrowly defined

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:39.060
and there's been a series of papers.

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.290
I'm gonna show you one,

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.820
but there have been a
couple of meta-analysis

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.023
that now show this.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.380
My colleague, Jamie McPartland her,

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:48.050
and I and Brian Reichow Who was in here,

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.390
is now an associate
professor down in Florida,

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.130
looked at the old DSM-IV
data, what we could find.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.100
About 30 cases have been lost in a flood.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.383
Let me warn you about winter around here.

00:13:58.905 --> 00:13:59.747
But we were able to recall these data

00:13:59.747 --> 00:14:01.540
and we went back and did, looked at them,

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.130
and then we programmed it in for DSM-V,

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.460
and we discovered a
very interesting thing.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.180
It was very specific, but the trouble was

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.428
who it left out.

00:14:10.428 --> 00:14:13.650
And here is what happened to the cases.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:14.720
And what you can see here is

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.200
among the high-functioning
people with autism,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.093
by the clinical diagnosis,

00:14:19.093 --> 00:14:22.750
a fair number, almost
50% lost their label.

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:24.890
But look at Asperger's and PDD NOS,

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.510
most of them lose their label.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:28.760
This was not happy news.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.960
This came out, this literally was featured

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.510
in the front page of the New York Times.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.800
And of course, what's the
problem in this country?

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.990
You lose your label, what do you lose?

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:40.320
Services, and so this was not happy news.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.570
So but this time, the DSM folks had more

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.200
or less wrapped it up and they
had to add a new criteria.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.340
Which was, if you already have
a well established diagnosis

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.901
of Autism, Asperger's,
PDD NOS, you can keep it.

00:14:51.901 --> 00:14:54.310
So that was the solution,

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.090
which is back to my train wreck business.

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:57.270
It's like you've got two trains

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.127
running on the same track.

00:14:59.127 --> 00:15:00.537
And it's problematic,

00:15:00.537 --> 00:15:03.210
and I'm sure this will
be addressed in DSM 5.1,

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.723
whenever it shows up.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.480
Let me say a little bit about
research and what we know.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.550
Genetics, we know there's
tremendous genetic factors

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.270
in autism, I'm gonna say something about

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:13.720
social brain interventions,

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:15.160
and a little bit to show you that

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:16.530
we think actually our interventions

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:18.633
are producing better outcomes.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.040
Research has increased in
terms of just literally

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.420
the number of papers and
the number of journals.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.300
I think there are over 20 journals now

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.330
that have autism in the title.

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:29.163
I'm the editor of the Journal of

00:15:29.163 --> 00:15:30.570
Autism and Developmental Disorders,

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:31.940
which is the first one.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.410
When I first took over,
now about 10 years ago,

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:36.943
we had about 300 papers come
in every year for review.

00:15:36.943 --> 00:15:41.090
I think last year we had
close to 1,500 papers,

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:42.680
which is just a tremendous number.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.340
And you can imagine,
I've gone from having me

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.570
and two associate editors to me

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.170
and about a zillion associate editors.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:49.920
But let me say a little bit about,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.810
this shows you the research early on,

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:53.560
and this is grouped by five year blocks,

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:54.950
except for that first block.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:58.540
So we can see that by 1990 to '95,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:01.630
we had over five years about 1,200 papers.

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.334
So that's a little over
1,200 divided by five,

00:16:05.334 --> 00:16:08.165
not so bad, you can keep up with that.

00:16:08.165 --> 00:16:10.720
But with what's happened more recently,

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.140
I don't have the '18 data yet.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.530
It takes a little while to get them,

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:19.530
but you can see there are
close to 4,000 papers a year.

00:16:20.298 --> 00:16:22.543
So how are you gonna keep up?

00:16:22.543 --> 00:16:24.530
I mean it's good news and it's bad news,

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:26.200
'cause it's a problem in
terms of just being able

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:27.503
to master the literature.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:30.010
Why has research increased?

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:30.900
Well, there's several reasons.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.008
First of all, there's a lot
of basic fundamental interest

00:16:34.008 --> 00:16:37.310
in Autism 'cause it's a protypical example

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:39.820
of a disorder involving the social brain.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:41.180
There's a whole body of work on this.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.269
I'm gonna show you a little bit.

00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:44.840
Borthers as the first one to
talk about this social brain

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:47.250
and really talked a lot about autism

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:49.810
and you're gonna hear a lot
here at the Child Study Center

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:52.110
about Social Neuroscience.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:53.770
And it seems also to be very interesting

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.800
because we can potentially bring the brain

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.400
and genes and behavior together

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:59.030
and understand more about fundamentally

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:02.070
what makes us as people social.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.490
What makes us so social?

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.140
There's also so social political reasons,

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:09.380
parent groups and foundations
have just tremendously

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:10.640
increased in terms of number

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:12.550
and their levels of support for research,

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:13.870
which is fantastic.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:15.911
Autism Speaks, the Autism
Science Foundation,

00:17:15.911 --> 00:17:19.160
the Simons Foundation
which is probably funding

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.920
more research than the federal
government is at the moment.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.580
But it's just really a tribute to the fact

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:25.740
that parents who used
to be afraid of talking

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:27.244
about having a child with autism,

00:17:27.244 --> 00:17:29.210
they say no, no, no,
we're gonna work on this,

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.180
we're gonna fix it, we're gonna tackle it.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:32.427
Which is fantastic.

00:17:32.427 --> 00:17:34.450
Finally there's financial reasons,

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:35.880
and people know Willie Sutton,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.250
people know who Willie Sutton was.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:42.250
He's a famous law in
medicine called Sutton's Law,

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:45.165
Sutton's rule, Willie
Sutton was a bank robber.

00:17:45.165 --> 00:17:47.760
And Willie Sutton, whose picture is here,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.910
they once asked Willie
why he robbed banks,

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:52.068
he said, "'Cause that's
where the money is."

00:17:52.068 --> 00:17:54.480
Well so the interesting thing here is,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.786
as money has increased in
terms of research, of course,

00:17:56.786 --> 00:17:58.810
people are on it.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:00.170
People that had previously no interest,

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:02.050
all of a sudden, they have
an interest in autism.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:04.370
And I'll talk a little
bit about the internet,

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.140
it's got good news and bad news,

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:07.270
but there's no question,

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:08.490
there's more media tension.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.886
I just saw something on Good
Morning America last week,

00:18:10.886 --> 00:18:13.213
which was a great ad for some lovely place

00:18:13.213 --> 00:18:18.213
that also talked about some
slightly unusual treatments.

00:18:19.067 --> 00:18:21.420
But for good news and bad news,

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:22.710
we've got the interest in this

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:24.790
and the internet will talk about it.

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:26.020
So understanding autism.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:27.400
What is autism?

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.100
And this movie did a lot to get people

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:30.985
in this country aware of autism, Rain Man.

00:18:30.985 --> 00:18:33.960
Autism is first and foremost a disorder

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.225
of social interaction,
including unusual patterns

00:18:37.225 --> 00:18:42.225
of learning and over engagement
with the non-social world.

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:46.450
So, what happens if you
have a social frame.

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.770
If you come into the
world and you're ready

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:50.384
to play the social game, right?

00:18:50.384 --> 00:18:52.480
What happens?

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:53.780
You pay attention to people,

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.405
you pay attention to
their faces, especially.

00:18:55.405 --> 00:18:58.250
You pay attention to
what they're looking at.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.730
You learn from just watching
what they're looking at.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:02.700
If something is dangerous or safe,

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.713
you look to your mom or dad,
is it dangerous or safe?

00:19:04.713 --> 00:19:06.850
You pay attention to their language.

00:19:06.850 --> 00:19:08.730
We were just talking
about language learning.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.820
Language becomes very interesting early on

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:14.360
'cause all kids produce a range of sounds,

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:15.460
some of which are not gonna be

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.670
in their language that they speak.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:19.920
But they lose those sounds
'cause they don't hear them.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.710
Kids with autism
sometimes have persisting,

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.300
very unusual sounds that
never would otherwise

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:26.400
be used in the English language.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:27.942
They develop prosody.

00:19:27.942 --> 00:19:31.187
Before nine, 12 months their
sing songs back and forth,

00:19:31.187 --> 00:19:32.930
it's English prosody.

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.970
They would prefer English
prosody if they're growing up

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:37.100
in an English speaking household.

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.500
So it's a whole, very
interesting package of stuff,

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:41.850
and we don't send children
to language school.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.060
That's one of the major proofs,

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.300
as I was talking with some
of my colleagues earlier

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:46.850
of the existence of God,

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:49.150
that children really do
learn language on their own

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:50.840
and they're very, very good at it.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.990
So even before the child is
saying their first words,

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:55.244
they're having this
protoconversations with their parents,

00:19:55.244 --> 00:19:57.730
they are having about
the same turn lengths,

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.336
they're going back and forth,

00:19:59.336 --> 00:20:00.670
there's an expectation of this is my turn,

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:01.630
this is your turn.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.430
This doesn't happen in autism,

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:06.780
but this happens if
you had a social frame.

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:09.310
I can remember my first child,

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:11.090
who just had our first grandchild,

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:12.427
when she was born she was two weeks late,

00:20:12.427 --> 00:20:14.629
it was a long labor and delivery.

00:20:14.629 --> 00:20:17.300
And my wife ended up having a C-section

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:19.340
and she spiked a temperature and,

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:20.290
because she was a pediatrician,

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:22.140
there's a zillion docs in the room,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:24.137
and so she has her C-section,
I check that she's okay

00:20:24.137 --> 00:20:26.500
and then go off to newborn special care

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:30.670
to see my new daughter who
has just been delivered.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:33.260
And I walk in and I say,
can I hold my daughter?

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:34.710
And of course there's
this big baby surrounded

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.110
by all the these tiny babies,

00:20:36.110 --> 00:20:37.640
and I hold her and she's looking at me

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:39.840
and I'm looking at her,
and we're off an running

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:44.840
and still are, and it's really
amazing to see this happen.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:48.850
So let me ask you a couple questions.

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.453
What's more interesting this or this?

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:55.970
I would argue that you
can't pull yourself away

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.014
from looking at the faces.

00:20:58.014 --> 00:20:59.980
And part of the reason for that is

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:02.642
we have a tendency as
humans to seek the face.

00:21:02.642 --> 00:21:04.562
So look at all these pictures.

00:21:04.562 --> 00:21:07.112
I would think you see a
face in all of them, right?

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:09.842
And none of these are human people.

00:21:09.842 --> 00:21:13.290
These are houses, these are bowling balls,

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:15.938
eggplants, but isn't it interesting?

00:21:15.938 --> 00:21:18.950
And so I say in some funny way

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:21.650
that for people with autism,
children with autism,

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:23.396
it's sort of like looking at this picture.

00:21:23.396 --> 00:21:26.833
How many Nave Seals are
hidden in this picture?

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.450
Take a look and then then make a guess

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:30.550
and then I'll show ya.

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:34.770
A ton, but think of what
if you came into the world

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.520
and you couldn't make out
people from the surroundings?

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:41.380
That's gonna cause some troubles.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:42.300
So to put it another way,

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:44.430
if you come into the
world with a social frame,

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.030
people are the center of your world.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.430
You're learning from them all the time,

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:49.400
you're focused on them all the time.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:51.550
You have something called Joint Attention.

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.890
Your mom looks at something,
you look at something.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:54.990
You follow what they're looking at.

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:57.147
You have incidental learning,
you have the development

00:21:57.147 --> 00:21:59.341
of affect and emotion and this will help

00:21:59.341 --> 00:22:01.910
to regulate how you
interact with your parents

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:03.530
and back and forth.

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:05.330
You also have a strong
desire to communicate

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:07.884
because it's so efficient in
getting your message across.

00:22:07.884 --> 00:22:11.080
And people are the most
important things in the world.

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:12.948
So again, in autism it's different,

00:22:12.948 --> 00:22:15.480
and we wanna know a little bit about

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.290
what we can understand about
social development early on,

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:20.030
especially in relation to the brain,

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:23.830
and how this affects children with autism

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:24.980
and their interaction.

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:26.769
So let me show you very quickly,

00:22:26.769 --> 00:22:29.170
again in typical development,

00:22:29.170 --> 00:22:31.780
there's a very strong
attraction to face from birth,

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:36.780
we are able to see very,
very clearly markers of this.

00:22:37.078 --> 00:22:39.887
Jamie McPartland, who
is on the faculty here,

00:22:39.887 --> 00:22:43.510
his first seminal work
was on looking at faces

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:46.490
and discovering a very
characteristic EEG phenomenon

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.340
in looking at faces, which
I'll show you in one second.

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:52.380
And here we go, here's Jamie and a baby

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:55.380
and here's someone doing the EEG,

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.610
which these are much
easier to do these days

00:22:57.610 --> 00:22:59.230
than when I first got into the field.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:01.411
You just wet their hair and
put on this little funny cap

00:23:01.411 --> 00:23:03.190
and you're off and running.

00:23:03.190 --> 00:23:06.224
But the interesting thing
Jamie has pointed out is this,

00:23:06.224 --> 00:23:08.010
when you average many of these things

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.450
for looking at faces, kids with autism,

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.613
kids without autism, you find this,

00:23:12.613 --> 00:23:15.740
and this is called the
N170 because it's negative

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.400
and it's 170 milliseconds out.

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:19.870
For people looking at faces,

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:22.253
there's a very characteristic response.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:26.480
And it's specific to faces.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:27.910
But look at the people with autism,

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:29.943
it's slower and it's not the same.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.400
So how can we understand this?

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:35.760
This is a study Bob
Shultz and a group of us

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:39.040
did some years ago looking
at faces versus objects.

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:41.527
Happily this has been
replicated many, many times now.

00:23:41.527 --> 00:23:43.936
We showed people lying
in the MRI machines,

00:23:43.936 --> 00:23:47.269
it's magnetic, no radiation,
one button for same,

00:23:47.269 --> 00:23:48.820
one button for different.

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.657
Same or different, same or
different, same or different,

00:23:50.657 --> 00:23:52.298
faces and objects.

00:23:52.298 --> 00:23:54.413
And the idea is we're
gonna compare and contrast

00:23:54.413 --> 00:23:57.533
where does the blood flow
go for objects versus faces?

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:02.060
And again, what we see
is for the typical folks,

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.010
there's an area called the Fusiform Gyrus,

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:05.970
which is right about here for most of us,

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:08.830
right under the ears on
the bottom side of the face

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:11.345
that is a specialized
facial recognition area.

00:24:11.345 --> 00:24:13.965
For people who are
typical, that lights up,

00:24:13.965 --> 00:24:15.990
we get more blood flowing there.

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:18.530
But not so for the people
with autism and Asperger's.

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:20.394
Again, these were high functioning people.

00:24:20.394 --> 00:24:23.620
Interestingly enough, we
had one of our students,

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:25.673
one of our trainees, we had one child

00:24:25.673 --> 00:24:28.306
who had a very strong interest
in something called Pokemon.

00:24:28.306 --> 00:24:32.887
For Pokemon, that area would light up.

00:24:32.887 --> 00:24:36.230
And the Pokemon was in contrast to faces,

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.033
including family member faces.

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.210
So again, it's not that
that area didn't work,

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:42.633
it didn't work with faces,

00:24:43.472 --> 00:24:45.813
which tells us there's something
different about the wiring.

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:48.520
Which is another very interesting thing,

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:50.953
this is a Yale undergraduate project

00:24:50.953 --> 00:24:53.540
from one of my students
in one of my classes

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.720
who came to me and said he wanted

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:56.340
to do this study on eye tracking

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:57.840
and I said, well I don't know.

00:24:58.757 --> 00:24:59.780
I'd seen an eye tracking
set up in the Netherlands

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:02.330
and you had to put the
person's head in a vice,

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:03.760
and you had to paste things around them,

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:05.110
and I'm like this is never gonna work

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:06.640
for a person with autism.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:08.334
He said, no no, the technology
has gotten much better

00:25:08.334 --> 00:25:11.137
and Ami Klin who was then here

00:25:11.137 --> 00:25:13.350
and I managed to raise a little money

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.624
to help him build this
$20,000 baseball cap.

00:25:16.624 --> 00:25:19.510
And at that point eye
tracking had not been done

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:22.750
in autism at all, it had been
done a little bit in children

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:24.490
for looking at eye movements

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.710
in reading problems and dyslexia.

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:27.920
It had been done in schizophrenia,

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:29.680
looking again at eye movements,

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:31.460
nystagmus, not so much in looking at

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:33.293
what the person was looking at.

00:25:33.293 --> 00:25:36.050
And so we looked at
this for the first time,

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:40.110
we took little five second
clips, very short clips

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:41.960
from a very intense movie.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:43.530
It involved really only four people.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.420
It was black and white
because we, at that point,

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:47.450
didn't know what color was gonna do to it,

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:50.504
and this is a nice movie
about a pleasant dinner party

00:25:50.504 --> 00:25:54.990
among four, two couples,
faculty at a New England college

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:58.910
called Who is Afraid of Virginia Wolf?

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.020
And for those of you
who have not seen this,

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:04.550
this is a very intense
movie and these people

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.119
just tear each other up, right?

00:26:06.119 --> 00:26:07.980
It's like a Greek tragedy,

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:10.174
but the interesting thing is

00:26:10.174 --> 00:26:11.470
we show little clips from this movie,

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:13.812
and here you can see the lovely
Emily Volkmar Demonstrating

00:26:13.812 --> 00:26:17.352
for $10, which she
negotiated up from five.

00:26:17.352 --> 00:26:20.610
And she's watching this,

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:24.364
she's got a very tiny
little camera on her hat.

00:26:24.364 --> 00:26:26.100
One camera is pointing that way,

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:27.670
one camera is pointing this way.

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:29.660
It's infrared so she can't see it,

00:26:29.660 --> 00:26:32.220
but we can actually track based on

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:33.580
what her retina is looking at,

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:35.120
the most sensitive part of the retina,

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.046
what's she really
focusing on in this scene.

00:26:37.046 --> 00:26:40.420
And I'm gonna show you data
from our first two people,

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:43.068
a man with autism who is
independent and self sufficient,

00:26:43.068 --> 00:26:46.767
but who very much has autism,

00:26:46.767 --> 00:26:49.183
and a typical viewer
who's not quite as bright.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:54.300
And here we go, this is about
five seconds of the movie

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.224
condensed onto a single frame, okay?

00:26:56.224 --> 00:26:59.310
At the top you see a typical viewer,

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:00.939
back and forth to the eyes,

00:27:00.939 --> 00:27:04.000
and the bottom you see
a person with autism,

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.360
back and forth to the mouth.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:10.062
Now the interesting thing, very quickly,

00:27:10.062 --> 00:27:14.060
as a total aside, when my
daughter is doing this,

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:16.062
we were doing this for a
German television crew,

00:27:16.062 --> 00:27:19.412
and the guys, so she did it,

00:27:19.412 --> 00:27:21.360
'cause they showed up
and said we want a child,

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:22.193
I was like okay I'll get you a child,

00:27:22.193 --> 00:27:23.810
we'll see if my daughter will do it.

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:24.643
And they said oh fine,

00:27:24.643 --> 00:27:26.350
this is wonderful, wonderful, wonderful!

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:27.855
Now show us you had autism,

00:27:27.855 --> 00:27:30.968
and she said, Dad what
am I supposed to do?

00:27:30.968 --> 00:27:33.797
And at this point we were
very early on in this,

00:27:33.797 --> 00:27:35.965
and I said sweetie, look at the objects.

00:27:35.965 --> 00:27:37.897
And so she immediately looks,

00:27:37.897 --> 00:27:39.420
and at this point, keep in mind,

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:43.570
I have seen this movie 100
times to pick out the clips.

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:45.650
What does she find in this
movie that I had never seen?

00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:47.330
See in the top corner,

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:48.750
there's a map of Martha's vineyard,

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:50.742
which I'm going to tomorrow.

00:27:50.742 --> 00:27:52.190
She had been going there all her life,

00:27:52.190 --> 00:27:53.540
I had been going since 1980,

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:55.670
I had never seen that map.

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:57.500
Because of course, I'm
looking at these people

00:27:57.500 --> 00:27:59.000
who are tearing each other up.

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:02.340
And the interesting thing,
here is a scene in the movie,

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.429
something scary has happened.

00:28:04.429 --> 00:28:07.270
It looks like the husband is
coming out to shoot his wife.

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:10.051
He's got a gun, pops
an umbrella, ha ha ha.

00:28:10.051 --> 00:28:13.390
Big joke in the room, but the
couple doesn't know that yet.

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:15.190
But you see where the typical viewer goes,

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:16.762
straight to the eyes?

00:28:16.762 --> 00:28:19.657
And the person with autism
goes straight to the mouth.

00:28:19.657 --> 00:28:21.680
The trouble with this is,

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.427
if your an eye person
versus a mouth person,

00:28:24.427 --> 00:28:26.950
the eye folks get about 90% of

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:28.793
the social affective information,

00:28:28.793 --> 00:28:30.653
the mouth is much less.

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:34.220
This actually has held up
pretty well in meta analysis.

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:36.734
The eye business especially,
the mouth business,

00:28:36.734 --> 00:28:37.910
there's a little more variability,

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:39.381
but I think that's probably more

00:28:39.381 --> 00:28:40.470
methodological than anything.

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:42.100
But in our first series of cases,

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:43.890
this is now a larger series of cases,

00:28:43.890 --> 00:28:46.280
you can see here, there's no overlap

00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:48.350
in terms of looking at the eyes.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.565
There's something Cohen's
D, which is an estimate

00:28:50.565 --> 00:28:54.000
of the effect size, the Cohen's D here

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.590
is three point something,
it's a whopping effect!

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:58.106
You know usually in treatment studies,

00:28:58.106 --> 00:28:59.964
we get a 0.7, 0.8, we
think oh this was great.

00:28:59.964 --> 00:29:03.430
But this is what we're against
in terms of the treatment.

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:05.533
It's the severity of the social problem.

00:29:06.770 --> 00:29:08.300
So what does all of this
mean in terms of outcome?

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:10.050
Well the interesting thing is,

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.210
as we've gotten more aware
of what the needs are

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:13.780
and the special intervention challenges,

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:15.770
but also needs to structured teaching,

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:17.693
we have gotten more effective programs.

00:29:17.693 --> 00:29:20.880
Increased awareness has also
led to earlier diagnosis.

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:22.660
You're gonna hear from Kasia,
we're are very interested

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.670
in autism in very young children,

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:26.417
and we think there is
probably now a better outcome

00:29:26.417 --> 00:29:30.010
as children have gotten earlier
and earlier interventions.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:31.640
I'm gonna show you some data on that.

00:29:31.640 --> 00:29:33.661
And we're interested, because
we think here especially,

00:29:33.661 --> 00:29:36.398
we wanna integrate clinical
work with research.

00:29:36.398 --> 00:29:38.620
Now let me point out
that there's some issues

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:40.180
in terms of outcome.

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:43.160
The early studies, people would
confuse with schizophrenia,

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:44.500
that was a problem.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.235
More recently there have been
some changes in the criteria.

00:29:46.235 --> 00:29:49.240
One of the big things that
happened in this country

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:53.005
was Public Law 94-142,
back in 1974, '75 I think.

00:29:53.005 --> 00:29:57.710
IDEA, law said any
child with special needs

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.990
who walks through the
door is entitled to a free

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:01.840
and appropriate education.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:03.740
This was a big game changer.

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:05.190
Autism was specifically mentioned,

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:07.620
all of a sudden schools
had to provide services.

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:09.600
Before this time, parents
could be told by the school,

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:11.150
we can't educate this child.

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:13.220
Go put them in one of
those nice trading schools,

00:30:13.220 --> 00:30:14.656
which is what happened.

00:30:14.656 --> 00:30:16.500
And so kids went to institutions,

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:17.730
they learned in institutions,

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.505
but they didn't get interventions,

00:30:19.505 --> 00:30:20.550
the kind that we know are effective.

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:24.090
And so this all started to
change with Public Law 94-142.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:27.510
There's all kinds of issues
in terms of the methodology,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.360
outcome, and if anybody's interested

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.362
we can talk about this if
we have time for questions.

00:30:31.362 --> 00:30:35.104
But I'm gonna sample some data,

00:30:35.104 --> 00:30:37.740
and I warn you, anytime somebody
warns you pay attention,

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:39.538
I'm cherry picking data.

00:30:39.538 --> 00:30:41.170
I'm cherry picking for a good reason,

00:30:41.170 --> 00:30:42.510
I'll tell you why in a second.

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:44.650
But these are data from Pat Howlin

00:30:44.650 --> 00:30:49.650
and Iliana Magiati's
review in 2019 in that book

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:52.120
I mentioned to you from
Cambridge University Press.

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:54.427
Good outcome means you're out there,

00:30:54.427 --> 00:30:56.760
you're on your own, your self sufficient.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:59.140
Fair outcome means you have
some reliance on other people,

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:02.150
usually your parents, but
you're checking in with people.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:03.400
So it's semi-independent.

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:05.680
And poor outcome is
you're totally dependent,

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:07.020
and we'll have chance to talk about

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:07.900
the really good outcomes,

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:10.086
because we do have some kids

00:31:10.086 --> 00:31:11.548
who have amazingly good outcomes.

00:31:11.548 --> 00:31:14.850
And these are the first outcome studies,

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:16.480
and the reason I cherry picked these,

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:18.056
this is going from '56 to '74.

00:31:18.056 --> 00:31:20.280
Why do I stop at '74?

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:23.195
Because Public Law 94-142 kicks in.

00:31:23.195 --> 00:31:26.180
So this is the first set of
studies, there's no mandates,

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:27.723
people are doing the best job they can,

00:31:27.723 --> 00:31:30.770
blah blah blah, but nobody
really knows what they're doing.

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:35.053
But look at the number of good
outcomes, maybe averaging 5%.

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:41.373
Okay, and poor outcome is
probably about 2/3 of cases.

00:31:41.373 --> 00:31:43.307
Now we're gonna skip ahead,

00:31:43.307 --> 00:31:47.640
and we're skipping ahead,
we're jumping 15 years

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:50.013
to let Public Law 94-142 kick in.

00:31:50.013 --> 00:31:52.820
I am going from '89 to 2003.

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:56.920
I stop at 2003 because 2004 we got DSM-IV,

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:58.719
we include Asperger's,

00:31:58.719 --> 00:31:59.700
that's gonna make things
look better anyway.

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:02.850
So that's the reason for
this sort of cherry picking.

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:04.950
But look at what's happened with the data,

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:08.433
the number of good outcome,
we're talking, 20, 25%.

00:32:08.433 --> 00:32:10.737
And that's because we're getting more

00:32:10.737 --> 00:32:13.120
and better interventions, and
there's good reason to think

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:14.880
that as we continue to do this,

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:16.440
as we provide interventions earlier,

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:18.340
we'll see even more of this.

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:20.540
And I'll talk about this again at the end.

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:22.340
So we think several factors are involved

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:24.154
in this change in outcome.

00:32:24.154 --> 00:32:25.960
First of all, the early
diagnosis and intervention,

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:28.092
we know have a number of
evidence based interventions.

00:32:28.092 --> 00:32:31.231
There have been some changes
in diagnostic practice,

00:32:31.231 --> 00:32:33.340
that might account for some of this.

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:36.330
As I showed ya, these data
were really before DSM-IV,

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:38.640
so that wouldn't account for most of it.

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:40.280
Clearly, not every child gets better.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:41.917
We have some children,
we can't predict who,

00:32:41.917 --> 00:32:44.120
and it frustrates the heck out of parents

00:32:44.120 --> 00:32:45.770
because a parent of a two
or three year old will say,

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:47.140
how's he gonna do, doc?

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:49.152
And you have to say, I can't tell ya.

00:32:49.152 --> 00:32:49.985
I can tell ya for groups of things,

00:32:49.985 --> 00:32:51.742
I can tell you these are
the things to look for.

00:32:51.742 --> 00:32:54.458
You will some children at
two, three, four years of age

00:32:54.458 --> 00:32:57.180
all of a sudden get it and
(imitates acceleration whoosh)

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.383
take off like a rocket.

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:01.741
And it's amazing to
see, but you do see it.

00:33:01.741 --> 00:33:05.512
On the other hand, you see
some children in good programs

00:33:05.512 --> 00:33:07.635
who just stay there, they are like a rock.

00:33:07.635 --> 00:33:09.830
And we can't predict really who.

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:12.010
It's possible as now we're
starting to use things

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:15.480
like EEGs and other things
to look at treatment effects,

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:17.060
that we may get better at seeing who does

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:18.760
and doesn't respond to treatments.

00:33:20.017 --> 00:33:20.950
It's a very interesting issue.

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:22.840
We have more and better supports.

00:33:22.840 --> 00:33:24.960
I mentioned, unfortunately, there's issues

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.360
of matching children to treatments.

00:33:26.360 --> 00:33:28.530
You have some children who might
be great candidates for ABA

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:30.200
but they're in TEACCH program.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.067
So you've got a lovely
program, but it's not ABA.

00:33:32.067 --> 00:33:33.670
And you might have some
kids who'd do better

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:35.660
in TEACCH program, but
they're in ABA program.

00:33:35.660 --> 00:33:37.710
So again, this issue of
matching kids and treatments

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:39.190
is very complicated.

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:40.810
There's a very good book by Peter Doehring

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:42.670
called Autism Across America,

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:44.920
where he talks about
some of these paradoxes.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.440
The interventions from '56 to 1980

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:51.160
were basically unstructured treatments.

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:53.634
We had some drug treatments, not so many.

00:33:53.634 --> 00:33:56.160
After Public Law 94-142,

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:58.050
there's all of a sudden great interest.

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:01.350
There was a report from the
National Research Council

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:03.647
called Educating Children
with Autism that came out.

00:34:03.647 --> 00:34:05.775
We're actually just redoing this,

00:34:05.775 --> 00:34:09.670
revisiting Educating Children with Autism.

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:11.480
This book from the
National Research Council

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:15.290
came out and said,
basically, educating children

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:17.740
with autism is important,
we see important changes,

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:19.640
it recommended 25 hours of treatment

00:34:19.640 --> 00:34:22.399
before kids went to preschool,
before kids went to school.

00:34:22.399 --> 00:34:23.940
So it was a big game changer,

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:25.837
there 10 programs around the country that

00:34:25.837 --> 00:34:27.840
had at least one published study

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:30.520
that showed that their program worked.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:32.500
But again, after that, there's been really

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:35.040
an explosion of evidence based treatments

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:36.846
and the programs have gotten much better

00:34:36.846 --> 00:34:39.571
as well as the evaluation of them.

00:34:39.571 --> 00:34:42.220
So we have more and
more books on evaluating

00:34:42.220 --> 00:34:44.019
evidence based treatments,

00:34:44.019 --> 00:34:45.360
there are practice guidelines out there.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.470
There's more stuff, we know that there are

00:34:47.470 --> 00:34:49.270
all kinds of approaches to teaching,

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:52.560
there are some programs like
TEACCH that are more eclectic,

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:54.951
and we have ABA, Applied
Behavior Analysis,

00:34:54.951 --> 00:34:57.100
we have some developmental programs,

00:34:57.100 --> 00:34:59.103
like Sally Rogers, Connie Kasari,

00:34:59.103 --> 00:35:01.340
that are more focused on fostering certain

00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:03.550
developmental areas and skills.

00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:05.210
And then finally we have pivotal response,

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:07.364
which is an interesting combination of

00:35:07.364 --> 00:35:11.120
behavioral and developmental,
and they pick certain areas

00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:13.177
that they see are pivotal
for enhancing development

00:35:13.177 --> 00:35:15.246
and they focus very much on those,

00:35:15.246 --> 00:35:18.413
but in kind of a more
naturalistic, ecological way.

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:21.870
So the gaps, intervention research,

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:23.787
and especially for older individuals,

00:35:23.787 --> 00:35:25.510
there's almost no work.

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:26.840
There was a paper some years ago,

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:28.699
Joe Pivot and some colleagues
that basically said

00:35:28.699 --> 00:35:31.820
there's no papers on aging in autism.

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:34.930
And if you read that paper,
you mastered the literature.

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:36.090
That's no longer the case.

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:39.340
We now have for the first time
some work going on on this.

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:40.650
It's a very interesting area,

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:41.900
but there's very limited data.

00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:44.238
We're generalizing a lot from our work

00:35:44.238 --> 00:35:45.931
with children and adolescents

00:35:45.931 --> 00:35:46.940
to our work with adults
and older individuals.

00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:49.080
There's no data looking at really older,

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:51.915
very little data looking at
older individuals and autism.

00:35:51.915 --> 00:35:53.500
For example, seizures.

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:55.850
We assume they may be at
increased risk for seizure.

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:58.130
I have a client who I took
care of in a group home

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:00.152
who is in her 60s and she
had her first seizure,

00:36:00.152 --> 00:36:02.840
and I got a call from
the ER up in Dartmouth

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:05.156
Where she was at the summer
place that they go to.

00:36:05.156 --> 00:36:07.020
And they said that she had the seizure

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.390
and we agreed that she
didn't need to be treated.

00:36:09.390 --> 00:36:11.590
She had only one seizure,
she wasn't driving equipment,

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:13.400
blah blah blah, and he said okay,

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:16.700
as soon as she starts to talk
I'll release her form the ER.

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:18.510
I said, well you know I've
known the lady for 25 years

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:19.911
and she's never said a word to me, so.

00:36:19.911 --> 00:36:21.240
(audience laughing)

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:22.560
Get ready for the long haul.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:25.510
But it's interesting we have no data

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:27.370
to show what the rates of seizures are.

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:28.820
If you look at something
like Downs Syndrome,

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:29.990
it's very different.

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:32.550
There's a lot of interest
in Down Syndrome aging

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:35.370
because of leukemia,
because of Alzheimer's.

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:37.572
And again, we just don't
know in autism, sadly.

00:36:37.572 --> 00:36:40.808
Particularly better metrics,

00:36:40.808 --> 00:36:44.470
things like looking at more
neurobiological things,

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:47.610
EEG, eye tracking things,
even to could have something

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:50.201
like turning response to prosody or voice

00:36:50.201 --> 00:36:52.970
that are much better than
our current approaches.

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:54.310
Our current approaches to screening,

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:56.900
I'm sorry to say, are
not horribly effective.

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.510
There was some data from Norway that shows

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:00.370
that the most commonly used instrument

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:02.320
works about as well as flipping a coin.

00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:06.640
And the trouble is it over picks
up developmentally disabled

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:09.340
and it under picks up for
more abled kids with autism.

00:37:11.285 --> 00:37:12.960
Also just to highlight this,

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:14.630
if you look, the gray bars are papers

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:16.030
being published in autism,

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:19.320
the black bars are papers
on intervention in autism.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.090
Intervention studies
are the hardest to do,

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:22.360
they're the hardest to get funded,

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.890
the hardest to get published.

00:37:23.890 --> 00:37:25.350
If you've ever been a reviewer

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:26.870
or will be a reviewer in the future

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:28.660
and you see an intervention
study come through,

00:37:28.660 --> 00:37:30.770
people are out, they are gunning for it.

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:32.160
Somebody in the review panel will say,

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:33.380
oh they didn't use my instrument.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:35.100
Oh, they used Joe's instrument.

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:36.960
And it all goes to heck after that

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:38.971
because people are involved
and they'll find some flaw,

00:37:38.971 --> 00:37:40.580
it's like an oriental rug.

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:43.070
It always has to have
some problem with it,

00:37:43.070 --> 00:37:44.557
and that usually kills it.

00:37:44.557 --> 00:37:45.850
The effects have been most effective

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:47.780
when they've mandated,
forced people really

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:49.980
to do a single study,
and that has actually

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:52.270
been more effective than other things.

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:55.210
Social policy issues, I
mentioned it's very interesting.

00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:57.450
We have essentially experiments going on

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:01.670
in all 50 states looking at
how autism is provided services

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:03.883
starting in young childhood and schools.

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:07.470
But nobody's really
considering what that is.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:09.470
It varies tremendously.

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.610
I'll sometimes be in the
position of advising people

00:38:11.610 --> 00:38:13.300
in this state to move
from one town to another

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:14.970
because they can get better services.

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:18.120
Other places I will say,
move out of such and such

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:21.087
and move across the river,
in a different state,

00:38:21.087 --> 00:38:22.980
you get much better services.

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:25.470
And it's a paradox and
nobody's looked at it,

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:28.187
and I really can't say,
there's no good reason why.

00:38:28.187 --> 00:38:30.260
As I mentioned, there's a
good book by Peter Doehring

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:32.667
called Autism America that
kind of takes up this problem.

00:38:32.667 --> 00:38:35.220
It's also been to some extent a problem

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:36.820
translating research into practice.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:39.870
As you know, anytime you
take a research intervention

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.930
out into the field, every single step

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:44.260
you're gonna get attrition
in terms of how effective

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:46.370
the practitioners are or
how well trained they are,

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:49.670
how faithful they are to the
protocol, blah blah blah.

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:51.410
But I think the other problem is,

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:53.381
that as researchers we
need to do a better job

00:38:53.381 --> 00:38:55.390
of taking what we know from research,

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:57.516
explaining it to people
in teachers in ways

00:38:57.516 --> 00:39:00.054
that they can find
helpful and approachable.

00:39:00.054 --> 00:39:03.300
If you look at the worldwide web,

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.116
I checked last night, there
were over 125 million hits

00:39:05.116 --> 00:39:07.788
if you type autism into Google.

00:39:07.788 --> 00:39:10.489
If you can imagine if
you're a parent doing that,

00:39:10.489 --> 00:39:12.229
what you're gonna be hit with.

00:39:12.229 --> 00:39:16.180
It's a lot, even among
the top 100 websites,

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:17.686
and there are several
published papers on this,

00:39:17.686 --> 00:39:20.390
about 1/3 of them are
gonna promise you a cure,

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:21.556
ask for money.

00:39:21.556 --> 00:39:25.116
Again, it's a problem that
parents really need help

00:39:25.116 --> 00:39:27.308
with finding good resources.

00:39:27.308 --> 00:39:30.230
Oh and I mentioned the online course,

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:33.420
this course, as you heard, this
lecture is gonna be online.

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:36.210
It's gotten a very substantial viewing,

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:38.370
which is great, we update it periodically.

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:40.130
But having good resources like that

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:42.260
available for free is an important thing.

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:43.500
Let me just mention very quickly

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:45.060
some general principles of the interaction

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:47.610
of research with clinical work.

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.867
I had as a patient a fairly
high functioning young man

00:39:49.867 --> 00:39:52.900
with several interests
who came to me saying

00:39:52.900 --> 00:39:54.050
he wanted a girlfriend.

00:39:55.091 --> 00:39:56.400
We got him two and a half years,

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:58.257
we got him a girlfriend, we got
them to go on a double date,

00:39:58.257 --> 00:40:00.020
we did a whole package of
stuff with them, it was great.

00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:03.294
And he came into my office
every day and he sat there,

00:40:03.294 --> 00:40:05.836
didn't look at me usually,
and we would talk.

00:40:05.836 --> 00:40:08.220
And about six months into it, I said

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:10.780
you know, Joe, I want you
to pretend I'm a girl.

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:12.360
And he looked at me, which was unusual.

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:13.870
He looked at me and said, Dr. Volkmar,

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:15.320
I can't pretend you're a girl.

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:16.560
You've got a mustache,

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:18.477
you've got white hair for christ's sake.

00:40:18.477 --> 00:40:20.118
(audience laughing)

00:40:20.118 --> 00:40:20.951
And he said, why would I do that?

00:40:20.951 --> 00:40:22.050
I said, Joe, if you can do it,

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:23.670
I will tell you what the girl is thinking.

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:25.050
And he looked at me with big eyes,

00:40:25.050 --> 00:40:26.010
you can do that?
(audience laughing)

00:40:26.010 --> 00:40:28.199
I said, yes.

00:40:28.199 --> 00:40:30.853
Anyway, after every
session he was going down

00:40:30.853 --> 00:40:32.520
and writing down rules.

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:35.440
Well, rule 157, Volkmar
says don't pick your nose,

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:36.820
girls don't like it.
(audience laughing)

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:39.110
And but he, we got him a girlfriend,

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:40.470
we got him to start a
chess club at school,

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:43.138
he got some friends, he
went on a double date

00:40:43.138 --> 00:40:46.880
with one of the guys from the chess club.

00:40:46.880 --> 00:40:48.900
And I said Joe, we're
gonna take you to Macy's

00:40:48.900 --> 00:40:50.714
to get you some new clothes
'cause you look like a geek.

00:40:50.714 --> 00:40:52.720
And the kid looks at me
and he says, Dr. Volkmar,

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:53.680
do I look like a geek?

00:40:53.680 --> 00:40:55.340
And I said, yeah you look like a geek.

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:58.330
And doing psychotherapy in this populace

00:40:58.330 --> 00:40:59.847
is very interesting.

00:40:59.847 --> 00:41:01.440
And he said, well how
can I look different?

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:03.112
And I said Joe, I'm
gonna tell you something

00:41:03.112 --> 00:41:04.160
that's gonna really freak you out.

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:05.477
And he said, what's that?

00:41:05.477 --> 00:41:07.660
And I said see my scissors over there?

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:09.490
We're gonna cut that top
button off of your shirt.

00:41:09.490 --> 00:41:11.090
Joe came in every time, no tie,

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:13.122
but all the buttons are buttoned, right?

00:41:13.122 --> 00:41:14.640
So, you'll forgive my saying,

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:16.253
he looks like a Trailing Missionary.

00:41:16.253 --> 00:41:18.359
And he looks very different
from his classmates.

00:41:18.359 --> 00:41:20.430
And he said, oh I can't do that.

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:22.187
I'll deface my shirt, my mom will kill me.

00:41:22.187 --> 00:41:26.227
And I said, Joe I'm
willing to bet $10, right?

00:41:26.227 --> 00:41:28.730
$10 that your mom's gonna like it.

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:30.425
Kids are gonna comment
that you look different.

00:41:30.425 --> 00:41:33.390
And so we did it, he came back
in the next time with $10.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:35.286
He says, I don't know how you knew that.

00:41:35.286 --> 00:41:38.292
But things like that, but
again he's very successfully

00:41:38.292 --> 00:41:42.510
enrolled so far, knock on wood,
in a college up in Boston.

00:41:42.510 --> 00:41:44.526
But the interesting
reason for telling this is

00:41:44.526 --> 00:41:46.565
while I was working with him,
he had my cell phone number,

00:41:46.565 --> 00:41:49.162
he also had an interest
among other things in

00:41:49.162 --> 00:41:51.820
the interchanges on interstate 95,

00:41:51.820 --> 00:41:54.381
you know South Carolina,
exit 63 is having problems

00:41:54.381 --> 00:41:56.710
at blah blah blah, and subways.

00:41:56.710 --> 00:41:58.350
He was interested in subways.

00:41:58.350 --> 00:41:59.690
And I had to cancel one meeting

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:02.077
because I had to be in Washington
for working on something

00:42:02.077 --> 00:42:04.060
for the National Research Council,

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:06.620
and it was horrible weather

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:08.401
and the train stopped a couple times.

00:42:08.401 --> 00:42:10.100
And I checked and called that the meeting

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:12.316
was actually gonna happen
'cause they were predicting

00:42:12.316 --> 00:42:14.472
a blizzard in Washington, which
just means it's impossible.

00:42:14.472 --> 00:42:16.690
So I get there, there's
six inches of snow,

00:42:16.690 --> 00:42:18.463
the city is shut down, you have to pay $50

00:42:18.463 --> 00:42:22.760
just to get into a cab, and I
slip and slide to the hotel.

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:24.206
The next morning there's no cabs,

00:42:24.206 --> 00:42:26.410
I have to go to the subway.

00:42:26.410 --> 00:42:28.321
I am getting on the
redline at DuPont Circle,

00:42:28.321 --> 00:42:30.100
having fought my way through the snow,

00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:32.004
fought my way through all the people,

00:42:32.004 --> 00:42:33.386
it's like the Tokyo
subways, pawing my way in,

00:42:33.386 --> 00:42:36.210
I get in the subway door, the door closes.

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:37.620
I get a text from my patient,

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:40.434
Doctor Volkmar, don't take the redline.

00:42:40.434 --> 00:42:41.437
(audience laughing)

00:42:41.437 --> 00:42:42.720
And he was absolutely right,
10 minutes later is stopped.

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:45.221
And there was some debris
on the track or something.

00:42:45.221 --> 00:42:47.379
But anyway, it's interesting,
every now and then

00:42:47.379 --> 00:42:50.250
you see these funny things that happen.

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:52.095
And lastly, quickly to
end with a happy note,

00:42:52.095 --> 00:42:54.115
Jamie McPartland has joined
me in teaching this class,

00:42:54.115 --> 00:42:56.647
we have done it now for over 30 years,

00:42:56.647 --> 00:42:58.414
and these Yale students who have a seminar

00:42:58.414 --> 00:43:00.920
and they have lectures, but
then they go out and work,

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:02.679
and the schools, Chapel
Haven and Benhaven,

00:43:02.679 --> 00:43:06.160
with people with Autism Spectrum Disorder.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.050
And it's an unusual course for Yale,

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:09.990
and as I mention, it's online.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:10.970
But to get into the course,

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:12.440
'cause it's limited to about 20 kids,

00:43:12.440 --> 00:43:14.190
they have to write me a little essay

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:16.440
and the essay is where they are at Yale,

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:18.860
why they're taking the
course, et cetera, et cetera,

00:43:18.860 --> 00:43:20.145
and they send it in.

00:43:20.145 --> 00:43:21.466
And I have a teaching fellow,

00:43:21.466 --> 00:43:22.797
and I say to teaching fellow,
okay this is the deal,

00:43:22.797 --> 00:43:24.559
first take the Seniors,
then take the Juniors,

00:43:24.559 --> 00:43:26.780
then take the Sophmores,
then take the Freshmen.

00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:29.580
But, if anybody says they're
a sibling, take them.

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:33.110
And very year I'll have a couple
of people who are siblings.

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:34.397
And I say, whatever else is true,

00:43:34.397 --> 00:43:36.257
they should get something
of being a sibling.

00:43:36.257 --> 00:43:37.767
And so I always take them,

00:43:37.767 --> 00:43:39.914
and a couple years ago now,
my teaching fellow said,

00:43:39.914 --> 00:43:41.170
you know what, Dr. Volkmar,

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:42.580
this is not quite what you said.

00:43:42.580 --> 00:43:44.332
But I want you to read
this student's essay,

00:43:44.332 --> 00:43:46.410
and I wanna take this because I wanna be

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:48.410
a better advocate for people with autism,

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:49.560
because I have autism

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:51.610
and I've never told anybody at Yale that.

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:54.830
And so I said, okay, the kid's in.

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:56.490
And I got to know him and yeah,

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:59.220
sure enough, I mean I'm dealing
with him as a professor,

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:01.890
not as a clinician, but I can tell ya,

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:02.723
he sure had autism.

00:44:02.723 --> 00:44:05.090
But it's very interesting, we
have more and more students.

00:44:05.090 --> 00:44:06.986
Last I heard, we have
five students at Yale

00:44:06.986 --> 00:44:09.247
at Southern where I
also work part time now,

00:44:09.247 --> 00:44:12.730
we have over I think 45
students who have identified,

00:44:12.730 --> 00:44:15.070
which means we probably have double that.

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:16.770
So more and more kids
are going to college,

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:18.700
it's creating some interesting challenges,

00:44:18.700 --> 00:44:20.340
but that's a whole different topic,

00:44:20.340 --> 00:44:22.610
and we're having a conference,
as you hear, this Fall

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:24.307
to address these transitional issues.

00:44:24.307 --> 00:44:26.249
But the good news is more and more people

00:44:26.249 --> 00:44:27.857
are doing better and better,

00:44:27.857 --> 00:44:31.250
and we have to now be able
to gear up to service them.

00:44:31.250 --> 00:44:34.130
So that being said, the take home lesson,

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:36.180
if you've met one person with autism,

00:44:36.180 --> 00:44:38.300
you've met one person with autism.

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:39.745
And so keep that in mind,

00:44:39.745 --> 00:44:41.690
and since this is Yale,

00:44:41.690 --> 00:44:46.527
I'd hope as a bulldog I
haven't put you to sleep.

00:44:46.527 --> 00:44:48.410
(audience laughing)

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:52.460
And finally, here's some references.

00:44:52.460 --> 00:44:54.260
I'll put number I up just for a minute

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:55.630
and then I'll go to number II,

00:44:55.630 --> 00:44:57.550
and then we'll be all done
and we can stop the filming.

00:44:57.550 --> 00:44:59.550
And we have a few minutes for questions?

00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:07.001
Whoops, okay all done.

00:45:07.001 --> 00:45:11.540
Thank you very much.
(audience applauding)

00:45:13.999 --> 00:45:16.666
(chiming music)

